      "section": "results",
      "text": "4 c-d). The general trend of lower PFAS recoveries in the 3rd cycle may be attributed to PFAS migrating to deeper pores of the resins over repeated use, increasing the difficulty in regeneration. The above results suggest that while both resins can be reused as long as they can still effectively capture PFAS from water, the incomplete regeneration over multiple cycles observed in both resins eventually will require resin replacement. Nevertheless, the treatment train still shows advantages in extending the resin service life and reducing waste production compared to the IX treatment alone. 3.4.3. Distillation of waste regenerant Distillation of waste regenerant removes methanol for the following UV/sulfite treatment, recycles methanol in the condensate, and con - centrates PFAS into the still bottoms. After distillation, methanol con - tents (60 % in the original regenerant) reduced to ~5 % in the still bottoms and increased to ~75 % in the condensates ( Table S8 ). PFAS concentrations in the still bottoms and condensate under different treatment conditions (water matrices, pristine/regenerated resins) were presented in Table S9 . Most PFAS were retained in the still bottoms, while little were detected in the condensate. PFAS concentrations in the still bottoms for IRA910 are significantly higher than those for PFA694E since more PFAS were recovered during IRA910 regeneration ( Figs. 3 \u2013 4 )."
